A||||6||superXmotorsports||stuck cap on clutch resvr||||5
Z||000000||superXmotorsports||11-28-1999||11:00 PM||||Any tips on getting the cap off to add fluid when it won't turn?  This is the 2nd Fiero I've had that the cap won't come off (without maybe twisting the entire thing apart)  I noticed it was slipping a little and it's fairly low - I hoping this will fix it.  Thanks!!!!||||reg||5
Z||000001||RZURCTN||11-28-1999||11:17 PM||||If your fluid is low, it may be causing a vacuum in your reservior.  Try cracking the line loose and see if that helps.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Patrick||11-29-1999||02:14 AM||||<BR>I was thinking of a vacuum also, but wouldn't it be easier to simply open the bleeder valve? Although if the system hasn't been bled for awhile, I'd force some fluid out of the valve first to clear any crap out of it. (You wouldn't want any crap to be sucked in, if it is a vacuum problem.)<P>However, I doubt that a low fluid level is responsible for your clutch slipping. That's unless your clutch disk is now worn out because you haven't been able to <B>disengage</B> your clutch enough due to a too low fluid level.<P>||||reg||4
Z||000003||Sage||11-29-1999||08:47 AM||||Had this problem on my sons' 88 coupe.  If you can get a pair of channel locks on the resivoir and hold it and another pair on the cap, it comes right off and doesn't break anything.||||reg||1
Z||000004||dogtired||11-29-1999||10:20 AM||||I have to agree with SAGE! That is the method I have to use on my 87, otherwise I will break something? 2 pair channel-locks, or waterpump pliers.||||reg||6
Z||000005||superXmotorsports||11-29-1999||11:10 AM||||Thanks everybody I'll try it and post the results...||||reg||1
Z||000006||superXmotorsports||11-29-1999||02:52 PM||||Good advice - I got them both right off.  <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  Unfortunately it didn't fix the clutch problem (and it's supposedly a new clutch)<BR>||||reg||1
